WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Your career starts the day you walk out of here. Let's make sure you're ready.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:45.000
First aid for HVAC-specific injuries: electrical shock (disconnect power first,
never touch the victim while energized), burns from brazing and hot surfaces
(cool with water, cover with sterile dressing), refrigerant frostbite (warm

00:00:45.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Heat Stroke: Life-threatening emergency. Hot red dry skin, confusion. Call 911
and cool immediately.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Heat Exhaustion: Heavy sweating, weakness, nausea. Move to shade, hydrate, rest.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Here's why this matters on the job: Knowing first aid for trade-specific
injuries can save your life or a coworkers life.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Watch out for these: Disconnect power BEFORE touching an electrical shock
victim. And — Heat stroke is a 911 emergency

00:02:37.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Take a minute before you move on. What's the one thing from this lesson you need
to remember on a service call?

